 ],
 "literal": "The beautiful washerwoman, having soaked in starch and lifted, / having beaten the ends and thrown into the cool pond, / resembling a thick wick unseparated from water, / the large-leaved pakantrai's bud-opening pure white flower, / which blooms without the fragrance of sweet, strong toddy, / even this sorrowful evening causes loneliness. / Is it today, O friend, in the land where he went?",
 "translation": "O friend, the pure white flower of the large-leaved pakantrai vine, opening from its bud, resembles a thick, wet lamp-wick thrown into a cool pond by a beautiful washerwoman who soaked it in starch, beat its ends, and washed it. Though it lacks the fragrance of sweet, strong toddy, it still blooms. Even this sorrowful evening brings loneliness here. Is it the same today in the land where he has gone?",
 "modern": "தோழியே! அழகிய வண்ணத்தி கஞ்சியில் நனைத்து, முனைகளை உதறி, குளிர்ந்த குளத்தில் துவைத்த ஈரமான தடித்த விளக்குத் திரியைப் போன்றது, பெரிய இலைகளைக் கொண்ட பகன்றைக் கொடியின் மொட்டவிழ்ந்த வெண்மையான மலர். அது இனிமையான, கடுமையான கள்ளின் மணம் இல்லாததாக இருந்தாலும் மலர்கிறது. இந்தத் துன்பம் தரும் மாலைப் பொழுதும் எனக்குத் தனிமையைத் தருகிறது. அவர் சென்ற நாட்டிலும் இன்று மாலைப் பொழுது இப்படித்தான் இருக்குமோ?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend during the evening, expressing her deep grief over the absence of her lover who has gone away on a journey. She looks at the white pakantrai flower blooming in the pond and compares it to a thick, wet cotton wick that a washerwoman has starched, beaten, and left in the water. Although the flower lacks the sweet, intoxicating scent of toddy, its sight in the evening triggers her sorrow. She wonders if this same lonely evening has descended upon the land where her lover is currently traveling. This poem belongs to the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, indicated by the pond, the washerwoman, and the pakantrai flower which grows near water bodies.",
